{"name":"Azure","hex":"#007fff","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Azure","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Azure"}],"entries":[{"title":"Azure (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Azure is the color between cyan and blue on the color wheel. It is often described as the color of the sky on a clear day."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Azulejos_Parque_Eduardo_VII-2.jpg/330px-Azulejos_Parque_Eduardo_VII-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azure_(color)","wikidata":"Q373058"},{"title":"Tincture (heraldry)","blocks":[{"text":"Tinctures are the colours, metals, and furs used in heraldry. There are nine tinctures in common use: two metals, five colours, and two furs. The metals are or and argent ; the colours gules (red), azure (blue), vert (green), sable (black), and purpure (purple); and the furs ermine, which is derived from the winter fur of a stoat, and vair, which is derived from the fur of a red squirrel. The use of other tinctures varies depending on the time period and heraldic tradition in question."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Tinctures_%28metals_and_colours%29.svg/330px-Tinctures_%28metals_and_colours%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tincture_(heraldry)","wikidata":"Q3404720"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg/500px-ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg/1280px-ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Chalcant